## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new, well-defined operator (Maj) within Kleene-like three-valued logic and requires concrete CSP reasoning with backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- a custom 3-ary Maj operator (majority with T/F bias)
- constraint propagation and backtracking in non-binary domains

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function maj(a,b,c){
  const t=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='T').length;
  const f=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='F').length;
  if(t>=2) return 'T';
  if(f>=2) return 'F';
  return 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend Maj to 4 inputs while preserving monotonicity?
- What is the impact of introducing additional unknowns on backtracking complexity?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about constraint propagation in a three-valued logic, including disjunctions of implications and backtracking in presence of Unknowns.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Constraint propagation
- Backtracking search
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B

## Code Example

```javascript
function evalImpl(a,b){
  // Kleene: return value of a→b as ¬a ∨ b
  // assume values: 'T','F','U'
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or = (x,y)=> x==='T'||y==='T'?'T': x==='U'||y==='U'?'U':'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

